this morning we are hearing from  criminal justice reform activist alice  marie johnson  about the black lives matter movement  the backlash over her participation in  the republican national convention and  if she plans to vote in the fall johnson  as you may have heard was released from  prison two years ago  when president trump commuted her life  sentence she had served almost  22 years 22 years for a non-violent  drug and money laundering crimes and  they were her first offenses  then last week the president granted her  a full pardon the day after  her very stirring speech at the  republican convention  some say you do the crime you do the  time  however that time should be fair and  just  we've all made mistakes none of us  want to be defined forever based on our  worst  decision alice marie johnson joins us  now after that speech alice good morning  it's really good to see you  good morning gail it's great to see you  again let me just say you got rave  reviews after that convention after your  speech at the rnc  what was that moment like for you it  felt so  surreal to me at first to be speaking  before the nation  about criminal justice reform and really  fulfilling the promise that i made to  the women and me and i left behind  that was a dream come true for me you  know because alex this is the thing  people felt your heart they felt your  sincerity  they felt your passion and your  commitment listen you started off by  saying  i was told that the only way i would  leave this prison was as a corpse  that is exactly what i was told i was  given a  life plus 25 year sentence without the  possibility of parole so  my situation seemed like a hopeless  situation  and i'm not alone gail there are so many  people who are just like me  who are serving draconian sentences that  don't make sense  okay so you have this high of the speech  you deliver the speech everybody says  alice marie johnson well done you leave  the speech  and then what happens to you well as i  was leaving  the um the white house there were  protesters outside of the white house  some started following me and they were  saying  really awful things it was shocking that  i was going to actually  experience that we can have a dialogue  together  but when you're screaming at me and  saying ugly things that's not  really getting the message through so as  i walked away  and they had dispersed i thought to  myself  what disrespect and  if these were my grandchildren acting  like that because these were young  people oh as young  i would be so upset to have  yeah young people acting like that but  you know i don't want to paint  all protesters with a broad brush and  say that everyone acts like that there  are there were some bad actors  but i'm putting that behind me because  we really  this criminal justice reform is my  mission  i have heard you say that you want that  to be the focus but you know how this  goes alice in the world of politics  people look at you they see you as a  woman of color  there is a lot of conversation about  donald trump's position when it comes to  the black community and some i've heard  this and i know you've heard it too  that you're being used that you're upon  uh what is she doing what do you say to  those kind of voices  i'm going to say to those voices i'm 65  years old gail  and don't no one tell me what to do i've  got my own mind i do what i want to do  and since i got my pardon papers i put  those pardon papers in my back pocket  and i'm free totally to do whatever i  want to do  i'll never forget the picture alice of  when you first got out and you got to  see your children and your grandchildren  when you came running out of the prison  i'll never forget that scene  so what's the difference for you  personally between the commutation  and then he recently gave you a full  pardon well the commutation meant that i  could rejoin my family  but i was still under the supervision  of the government i still had to report  to a probation officer  i still had trouble restrictions so now  a pardon means that i'm free  to register to vote i'm free to travel  without checking in with anyone else  i am totally free a pardon means just  that  i've got my whole life back now i want  to get your thoughts  on the black lives matter movement  because really  stories like yours is what the black  lives matter movement is about  you know the the injustice in the  criminal  system what is your take on the black  lives matter movement today  well my my take on it we i i'm as a  black woman  i also want justice i also want justice  but i also want to feel safe but at the  very core  of myself as a black woman as other  black americans we want justice we want  to be heard  that i i just hope that we can be heard  in a way  that the messaging won't be mixed up  will you be able to vote in this  election ally so this is  i i'm hoping that i will be as soon as i  go back home  i'm going to find out if i can register  to vote  and i am going to register asap  i've been waiting for this day for 20  some years to be  fully restored to to be the citizen that  i  want to be to be able to exercise my  right to vote i know it's a personal  question but will you be voting for  donald trump  you can be assured i will be voting my  country's gail  and i will suggest that everyone vote  their conscience  thank you alice marie johnson thank you  for taking the time  cheering you on always it's really good  to see you thank you  good to see you too alice marie johnson  she's one of a kind i really admire her  listen she's smart she's savvy she  admits i've made some mistakes  she has certainly more than paid the  price for that and now she's devoted her  life to helping others  and the protesters she said it was black  and white and she said but i don't care  what color they were  she thought that they were wrong and  she's reaching across the aisle she says  to work with democrats and republicans  on this issue  because she said it's not about politics  for her it's about people yeah and  people matter but there were some  landmines in those questions and she  walked through without  touching off anything she's a she's a  very elegant intelligent woman  and when you see that you just think  that sentence they gave her was insane  anthony you look at it and you think i  must be reading this wrong first offense  and she gets  when she said she was told the only way  your family will see you as a corpse  so for her she said to be walking out of  prison and to be  able to see her children grandchildren  you also see she's got a great sense of  humor she said you know gail i have  something in common with  mother teresa martin luther king and  jesus christ what is that alice  well they all have haters and i have  haters too but i'm not going to let the  haters stop me from what i'm doing  somebody's always going to criticize  also knock out vote your conscience yes  yes  good advice

